WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.590
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by the CuriosityStream &amp; Nebula bundle.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:10.919
Watch the latest episode of our new series
“The Battle of Britain” on Nebula for

00:00:10.919 --> 00:00:11.919
just 14.79 a year.

00:00:11.919 --> 00:00:19.660
In 1969, a pioneering vision of the future
made its debut to the public.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:30.419
In a few years time, the sight of the concorde
on international airfields all over the world

00:00:30.419 --> 00:00:32.619
will be common place if all goes well.

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:36.460
Then there will be 130 passengers on board.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.859
Flying times will be cut iby half.

00:00:38.859 --> 00:00:44.140
London to New York from 7 hours 40 minutes
to 3 hours 20 minutes.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:49.050
The juxtaposition between this dated footage
and the futuristic plane is strange.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:55.590
It’s hard to believe passengers, 40 years
ago, in a time when colour TV was a luxury,

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:59.399
were flying across the Atlantic at the speed
of sound.

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:02.250
The Concorde was well ahead of its time.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.880
An anomaly in the history of commercial travel.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:12.350
A byproduct of misguided government funding,
a joint effort between Britain's BAC and France’s

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:17.770
Aerospatiale, and it’s development funded
entirely by the British and French governments

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:21.160
to the tune of 2.8 billion dollars.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:22.160
[1]

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.270
The Concordes legacy is marred by its commercial
failure, but this engineering marvel was a

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.850
triumph of ingenuity.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:35.220
Nothing remotely close to it had flown before,
and nothing like it has flown since.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:42.520
Turning what had been a military only technology
into a luxury flying experience required innovative

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:43.520
thinking.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:54.290
This is the insane engineering of the Concorde.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:02:10.540
Intro sequence

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:15.250
The engineers of the Concorde had several
unique challenges.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:20.810
Developing a supersonic plane is difficult
enough with military design requirements,

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:26.400
but developing a supersonic plane that would
satisfy the wealthy customers it intended

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.709
to serve is an entirely different ball game.

00:02:30.709 --> 00:02:36.079
Aerial refuelling makes fuel efficiency a
secondary concern for the military.

00:02:36.079 --> 00:02:41.909
The comfort of the plane's passengers is an
afterthought at best, and the price tag attached

00:02:41.909 --> 00:02:47.829
to these often limited-run speciality planes
is beyond the budget of a commercial airline.

00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:51.999
Those needs are reflected in the Concordes
unique design.

00:02:51.999 --> 00:02:55.810
A sleek exterior, hiding powerful engines.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:00.810
Engines developed from the iconic British
war plane, the Avro Vulcan.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:06.670
Fuel efficiency and supersonic flight are
two opposing ideas, but these engines, the

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:14.079
Rolls Royce Olympus 593, when flying at Mach
2, were the most efficient engine ever created.

00:03:14.079 --> 00:03:15.079
[2]

00:03:15.079 --> 00:03:20.719
These engines, when introduced for the Avro
Vulcan, were capable of generating just 49

00:03:20.719 --> 00:03:26.310
kilonewtons of thrust (11,000 lbs), but over
the course of their adaption for use in the

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:32.760
Concorde, increased in thrust by over triple
to a whopping 169 kN (38,000 lbs).

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:33.760
[3]

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:40.069
20% of this thrust increase was provided by
newly fitted afterburners, a technology typically

00:03:40.069 --> 00:03:43.469
reserved for military planes.

00:03:43.469 --> 00:03:48.340
Afterburners work by injecting fuel directly
into the superheated and high-pressure exhaust

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.510
of the turbine section.

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:54.769
Despite this air’s journey taking it through
the combustion chamber, only about half of

00:03:54.769 --> 00:03:57.269
the oxygen in the air is consumed.

00:03:57.269 --> 00:04:01.510
Leaving untapped energy in the exhaust of
the engines.

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:07.299
To capture this energy fuel can be injected
directly into the exhaust of the turbines

00:04:07.299 --> 00:04:08.519
and ignited.

00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:14.290
Basically acting like a rocket stage of the
engine, causing further expansion and acceleration

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:17.170
of the exhaust through the exhaust nozzle.

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:21.340
However this system is useless without a controllable
nozzle.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:27.540
Typical airliner engines have a fixed nozzle,
because these aircraft are expected to be

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:31.780
efficient at a relatively low range of subsonic
speeds.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:37.530
But the Concorde, with its massive range of
speeds, needs not one, but two controllable

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.620
nozzles to adequately control the engine's
power.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:46.650
The primary nozzle connects directly to the
jet pipe’s exit and consists of petal-like

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:52.890
structures that can close or open to vary
the diameter and therefore the area of the

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.400
jet’s exit.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:59.890
When the afterburner, or reheat as the British
engineers called it, was ignited, it caused

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:04.370
a massive increase in pressure at the engine's
exhaust.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:09.350
This increase in pressure could cause choked
flow in the engine, where a high pressure

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:15.410
zone ahead of lower pressure zones will prevent
air from flowing through the engine correctly.

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:21.130
To prevent that, the primary nozzle opens
wider when the afterburner is active, to decrease

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:26.020
pressure and ensure the mass flow rate through
the engine is unchanged.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:27.020
[4]

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:31.910
However, at times we do want to affect the
flow upstream of the nozzle.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:36.310
The Olympus 593 was one of the world’s first
two spool turbojet engines, predated only

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:41.290
by the Pratt and Whitney J57 engine, by just
a couple of months.

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:47.500
Two spool means the engine contained two compressor
sections, driven by two turbine sections,

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:53.400
each acting on their own concentric drive
shafts [3] Allowing the two sections to operate

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.140
at different rotational speeds.

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:56.140
The high pressure compressor was driven by
the high pressure turbine, and the low pressure

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:57.140
compressor was driven by the low pressure
turbine, which was the closest turbine to

00:05:57.140 --> 00:05:58.140
the primary nozzle.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:01.150
When the primary nozzle was actuated it caused
a pressure change across the low pressure

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:03.740
turbine, which would cause the low pressure
compressor to spin faster or slower.

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:09.840
This provided a method of engine control that
allowed the plane to optimise it’s performance

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.500
across its wide speed range.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:16.220
The secondary nozzle function is just as complex.

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:21.790
The eyelid doors look like some kind of steampunk
blast shield, and for good reason.

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:27.320
The force these secondary nozzles had to endure
influenced their appearance.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:33.640
Withstanding huge forces at both supersonic
and subsonic speeds, they could even close

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:38.050
completely on landing to function as reverse
thrusters. [4]

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:43.150
By actuating these nozzles the plane could
carefully manipulate the exhaust velocity,

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:49.910
and thus maximise thrust, because, together
the pair acts as a controllable converging

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:52.070
diverging nozzle.

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:56.920
Converging diverging nozzles are typically
used in rocket nozzles to accelerate the combustion

00:06:56.920 --> 00:07:00.870
products to the optimum speed and pressure.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:06.440
The opposite shapes are needed because air
acts completely differently at subsonic and

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:08.400
supersonic speeds.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:14.940
For example, when subsonic air travels through
a converging duct, it speeds up as the cross-sectional

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:21.000
area decreases until some critical area which
is referred to as the throat.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.000
This is where Mach one would be reached.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:29.820
At this point no further acceleration of air
can occur, as the flow is choked.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:37.091
Now, as you would expect, if that same subsonic
air flows through a diverging duct, the air

00:07:37.091 --> 00:07:40.660
slows down as the duct area increases.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:43.300
This is Bernoulli's principle at play.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:48.820
The bizarre thing is, the exact opposite occurs
when air travels at supersonic speeds.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:56.050
A converging duct will result in a decrease
in velocity and a diverging duct will result

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:57.720
in an acceleration.

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:04.200
So, in order to maximise the acceleration
of our exhaust gases we want to pass our subsonic

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:11.340
air from the engine first through a converging
nozzle, the primary nozzle, until it reaches

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:13.240
mach 1 at the throat.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:19.420
Then we want it to pass through a diverging
duct, the secondary nozzle, at supersonic

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:22.610
speeds, so it can continue to accelerate.

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:28.200
These pair nozzles allowed Concorde to carefully
optimise the nozzle profile for different

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:29.570
flight conditions.

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:31.860
A marvellous piece of engineering.

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:35.319
The same principles were used for the inlet.

00:08:35.319 --> 00:08:38.320
No engine can operate with supersonic flow.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:43.659
The shockwaves that would form inside the
engine would cause all kinds of chaos.

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:51.840
So, the Olympus 593 needed some way to decelerate
the supersonic air entering its unique square

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:52.990
inlets.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:59.560
These squared inlets were designed to simplify
the complicated task of creating nice laminar

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:03.730
subsonic flow for the engine's compressors.

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:09.009
Inside were a series of ramps and doors that
could open and close depending on the flight

00:09:09.009 --> 00:09:11.930
regime the intake was encountering. [5]

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:17.819
At take off the engine required all the air
they could get to accelerate the plane enough

00:09:17.819 --> 00:09:22.709
to get those cumbersome delta wings to generate
enough lift to take off.

00:09:22.709 --> 00:09:28.999
To funnel as much air as possible into the
engine, the two variable ramps were retracted

00:09:28.999 --> 00:09:30.860
into their closed position.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:36.220
A spill door in front of the engine intake
was held open to allow additional airflow

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:41.930
into the engine, while two bypass doors which
controlled the air around the cooling passage

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:44.480
around the engine were also closed.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:45.480
[6]

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:50.920
The afterburners were also active during takeoff,
but soon after the Concorde was required to

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:56.329
enter a noise abatement stage of flight where
the afterburners were turned off and power

00:09:56.329 --> 00:09:59.389
was reduced as the plane climbed.

00:09:59.389 --> 00:10:04.399
At this point the bypass doors open up to
allow for cooling air to travel along the

00:10:04.399 --> 00:10:06.240
outside of the engine.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:12.019
As speed increases the primary and secondary
nozzles adapt, gradually beginning to form

00:10:12.019 --> 00:10:18.389
the ideal converging diverging nozzle for
supersonic cruise Parallel to these changes

00:10:18.389 --> 00:10:24.490
in the nozzles the inlet ramps actuate to
create a converging inlet that slows down

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:28.649
and compresses the supersonic air entering
the engine.

00:10:28.649 --> 00:10:34.329
The fuel consumption of supersonic flight
is massive,but that is primarily driven by

00:10:34.329 --> 00:10:37.920
aerodynamic drag, not thermal efficiency.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:43.420
This supersonic air being rammed and compressed
into the engines helps the engine immensely

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:47.209
in achieving it’s high thermal efficiency.

00:10:47.209 --> 00:10:51.980
Thermal efficiency in jet engines is largely
determined by the pressure ratio achieved

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:53.180
by the engine.

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:58.210
That is the ratio of pressure between the
inlet of the engine and the outlet of the

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:03.610
compressor, which in the case of the Concorde
flying at cruise is 80:1.

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:05.949
An incredibly high pressure ratio.

00:11:05.949 --> 00:11:13.190
The highly efficient GEnX engines of the Boeing
787 only manage 58:1.

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:20.760
The Olympus 593’s two spools each contained
7 compressor stages, each driven by a single

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:22.399
stage turbine.

00:11:22.399 --> 00:11:28.170
The low pressure and high pressure compressors
together provided a pressure ratio of 14:1,

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:34.649
a moderately high pressure ratio that would
provide moderate efficiency at subsonic speeds.

00:11:34.649 --> 00:11:41.540
However, the pressure ratio multiplied to
80:1 at cruise as the supersonic air rammed

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:45.339
into the unique square inlet engines.

00:11:45.339 --> 00:11:51.139
This elevated pressure ratio resulted in extreme
temperatures inside the engine that necessitated

00:11:51.139 --> 00:11:56.949
the use of high temperature alloys that are
typically reserved for the turbine blades.

00:11:56.949 --> 00:12:02.100
Titanium blades were used for the early compressor
stages and high temperature nickel alloys

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:05.140
used in the even hotter later stages.

00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:06.140
[7]

00:12:06.140 --> 00:12:10.060
Cooling the drive shaft bearings became another
engineering challenge.

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:14.089
Increasing the mass flow rate of cooling oil
was not enough to keep the bearings within

00:12:14.089 --> 00:12:16.750
a safe operating temperature.

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:22.139
Looking at the differences between the original
Olympus engine used in the Avro Vulcan and

00:12:22.139 --> 00:12:27.420
the Concordes, hints towards some changes
the engineers were forced to make.

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:33.380
A centre bearing located immediately after
the high pressure compressor here no longer

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:40.440
exists in the Olympus 593, and the drive shaft
proceeding it takes a bottle-like shape, increasing

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:44.129
in diameter in the new gap between bearings.

00:12:44.129 --> 00:12:49.839
This centre bearing had to be removed because
the temperatures were too high in this location

00:12:49.839 --> 00:12:55.300
directly after the high pressure compressor,
but without the support of the bearing, the

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:57.790
drive shaft became too flexible.

00:12:57.790 --> 00:13:03.660
To accommodate this change, the drive shaft
needed to increase in diameter to increase

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:04.930
stiffness. [7]

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:09.459
The Concorde is full of strange little design
details like this.

00:13:09.459 --> 00:13:14.769
Solutions to unique engineering problems caused
by the immense cruising speed.

00:13:14.769 --> 00:13:20.100
In order to decrease fuel consumption as much
as possible, the front area of the plane needed

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:21.839
to be minimised.

00:13:21.839 --> 00:13:27.180
Delta wings allow wing spans to be decreased
dramatically and reduce the drag created in

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:28.490
supersonic flight.

00:13:28.490 --> 00:13:33.480
However, it creates some major issues for
low speed flight, where they struggle to generate

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:34.819
enough lift.

00:13:34.819 --> 00:13:37.540
Making takeoffs and landings difficult.

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:42.290
This is where the ogival delta wings of the
Concorde come into play.

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:47.420
This is a form of compound delta wing where
there are two sections to the wing, with the

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:52.579
forward section having a higher sweep angle
than the rear section, connected with an ogee

00:13:52.579 --> 00:13:57.269
curve going from concave to convex curves
smoothly.

00:13:57.269 --> 00:14:02.769
This shape allowed the Concorde to generate
additional lift and low speeds through the

00:14:02.769 --> 00:14:05.170
use of vortex lift.

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:10.420
When flying at high angles of attack, separated
air flow would roll over the wings to form

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:17.639
two stable cone shaped vortices where air
speeds were high and air pressure was low.

00:14:17.639 --> 00:14:19.860
Effectively creating lift.

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:25.610
However those high angles of attack needed
for low speeds created some serious visibility

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:31.350
problems for the pilots, whose cockpit would
have a lovely view of the sky if not for the

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:33.589
iconic droopsnoot.

00:14:33.589 --> 00:14:36.380
The droop snoot is a one of a kind solution.

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:42.129
A mechanised nose that could drop by 12.5
degrees to provide the pilots a clear view

00:14:42.129 --> 00:14:46.709
of the runway during these high angle of attack
manoeuvres. [8]

00:14:46.709 --> 00:14:51.379
The mechanism had four positions, controlled
by the pilot with this lever.

00:14:51.379 --> 00:14:56.790
The top position was the fully retracted position
used in supersonic flight.

00:14:56.790 --> 00:15:01.720
The second position lowered a specialised
heat shield visor used to protect the cabin

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:04.939
windows from the heat of supersonic flight.

00:15:04.939 --> 00:15:10.379
When placed in this position the visor hydraulic
actuator would retract, causing the visor

00:15:10.379 --> 00:15:13.230
to retract into the nose fairing.

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:18.790
The next position was the 5 degree down position,
used for takeoffs and taxis.

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:20.410
Extending this hydraulic actuator.

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:26.670
And the final setting pushes the nose down
further into the 12.5 degree setting needed

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:28.100
for landings.

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:33.579
The plane also had an extendable tail landing
gear, which on most take-offs and landings

00:15:33.579 --> 00:15:39.000
was not needed, but was present to protect
the plane's engines, which would be the first

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:44.529
point of contact for what would normally be
a tail strike for other airliners.

00:15:44.529 --> 00:15:49.160
Tails strikes aren’t all that uncommon,
usually happening on landings in difficult

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:50.319
conditions.

00:15:50.319 --> 00:15:56.430
Some planes, like the 777, also have extendable
protection measures in the form of tail skids,

00:15:56.430 --> 00:16:02.959
as a strike can at best force a plane into
maintenance costing the airline company money,

00:16:02.959 --> 00:16:08.350
or at worst cause serious damage to the pressure
bulkhead and put the lives of the passengers

00:16:08.350 --> 00:16:10.379
aboard at risk.

00:16:10.379 --> 00:16:16.209
In fact a tailstrike is responsible for the
crash that claimed the most lives out of any

00:16:16.209 --> 00:16:18.829
aviation single crash in history.

00:16:18.829 --> 00:16:26.670
In 1985, a Japan Airlines 747 flight crashed
in the mountains of Japan, 7 years after a

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:30.489
tail strike had occurred, which had not been
correctly repaired.

00:16:30.489 --> 00:16:36.110
With the high angles of attack demanded by
the Concorde for landings, this protection

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:39.130
was vital to keeping the plane safe.[9]

00:16:39.130 --> 00:16:44.040
The visor was needed during supersonic flight
because the nose of the plane experienced

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:47.320
temperatures of around 130 degrees celsius,

00:16:47.320 --> 00:16:53.660
while cruising at an altitude of 60,000ft,
where ambient temperatures are usually around

00:16:53.660 --> 00:16:56.230
-56 degrees celsius (or -70 degrees fahrenheit).

00:16:56.230 --> 00:17:01.470
The Concorde went through several design iterations
with this forward-facing protection.

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:06.699
The earliest prototypes simply removed the
lower forward facing windows completely.

00:17:06.699 --> 00:17:13.439
[12] But it turned out that pilots like having
a view of where they are going, so this wasn’t

00:17:13.439 --> 00:17:15.959
an option for the production model.

00:17:15.959 --> 00:17:22.189
However, a version of the droopsnoot with
a fully metal heat shield was originally considered

00:17:22.189 --> 00:17:27.269
for the Concorde, which would have eliminated
forward visibility during cruise which was

00:17:27.269 --> 00:17:33.450
considered non-essentially, but thankfully
these heat resistant glass visors were used

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:35.179
in the final version.

00:17:35.179 --> 00:17:40.700
Working with such high temperature variations
between takeoff and landing causes some serious

00:17:40.700 --> 00:17:46.549
structural issues, especially with the frequency
of flights needed to make a commercial plane

00:17:46.549 --> 00:17:48.800
financially viable.

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:54.400
The thermal expansion caused by these temperatures
made the plane grow by 20 centimetres (8 inches)

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:57.110
on each and every flight.

00:17:57.110 --> 00:18:02.880
The fatigue this thermal stress could cause
would eventually result in catastrophic failure

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:04.630
if not properly accounted for.

00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:10.080
The first issue was finding a material capable
of dealing with this heat.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:16.230
Titanium, which was used for the SR-71, could
have been used, but due to its cost and weight

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:18.559
was ruled out early.

00:18:18.559 --> 00:18:23.990
Traditional aluminium would not be capable
of withstanding these conditions, but a special

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:30.790
aluminium alloy, Hiduminium RR58, developed
in Britain during World War 2 for the newly

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:33.700
invented gas turbine engines, could.

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:34.700
[10]

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:41.149
The alloy consists of copper, magnesium, silicon,
iron, Nickel, and titanium with the remaining

00:18:41.149 --> 00:18:43.700
percent being made up of aluminium.

00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:48.520
Although this material had been developed
decades prior to the Concorde, it had never

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:51.120
been used in this application.

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:57.640
With lower average temperatures than its traditional
use inside gas turbine engines, but much longer

00:18:57.640 --> 00:18:59.269
life requirements.

00:18:59.269 --> 00:19:03.980
The parts would also be manufactured using
different techniques, like the cold rolled

00:19:03.980 --> 00:19:06.640
sheets needed for the fuselage.

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:13.049
This one process alone affected critical material
properties that improved heat resistance

00:19:13.049 --> 00:19:18.850
Forged parts tend to have larger crystal grain
sizes than rolled components, which helps

00:19:18.850 --> 00:19:24.370
forged components resist the internal crystal
structure from “creeping” when under stress

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:25.769
and heat.

00:19:25.769 --> 00:19:30.820
To adapt this material for the cold rolled
skin of the Concorde, the engineers at Rolls

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:37.470
Royce had to develop new manufacturing methods
to maximise the grain size of these cold rolled

00:19:37.470 --> 00:19:39.200
components.

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:43.450
Manufacturing knowledge that has no doubt
been used by Rolls Royce for more modern day

00:19:43.450 --> 00:19:44.450
applications.

00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:45.450
[10]

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:50.110
Now that a suitable material had been developed,
shaping it in a way that allowed expansion

00:19:50.110 --> 00:19:51.799
was crucial.

00:19:51.799 --> 00:19:57.090
Most of this expansion was imperceptible to
the crew and passengers of the Concord.

00:19:57.090 --> 00:20:01.780
Interior panels could slide past each other
to hide the expansion to the passengers, and

00:20:01.780 --> 00:20:08.019
crucially prevented stress forming as a result
of uneven expansion between the hot exterior

00:20:08.019 --> 00:20:10.159
and cool interior.

00:20:10.159 --> 00:20:14.950
Wiring around the plane was given slack to
prevent the wiring from snapping as the plane

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:20.100
expanded, and many components around the plane
were corrugated, meaning parts were given

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:21.580
a curved shape.

00:20:21.580 --> 00:20:26.269
This allowed these parts to stretch and flatten
the curves like a spring.

00:20:26.269 --> 00:20:31.370
However, one expansion gap was quite obvious
to the crew of the Concorde.

00:20:31.370 --> 00:20:36.299
As the flight approached its cruise speed
a gap between the flight engineer's console

00:20:36.299 --> 00:20:38.910
and the cockpit bulkhead began to form.

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:45.639
A gap large enough to slide your hand inside
of, or as became tradition for retiring Concordes,

00:20:45.639 --> 00:20:50.000
the flight engineer's cap would be placed
inside the gap as it closed.

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:52.750
Leaving it permanently wedged.

00:20:52.750 --> 00:20:58.610
Like the SR-71, this continual expansion and
contraction cycle caused some serious issues

00:20:58.610 --> 00:21:00.730
with fuel tank sealants.

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:05.850
In order to deal with the expansion cycles
a sealant needs to be flexible.

00:21:05.850 --> 00:21:10.820
Even the mundane plastic sealants on household
windows need this quality, to prevent small

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:13.690
movements from allowing drafts getting through.

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:19.820
The issue is that high temperatures tend to
harden sealants over time and then as the

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:25.919
expansion cycles continue the sealants begin
to crack and allow fuel to spill out.

00:21:25.919 --> 00:21:31.710
While the temperatures the Concorde experienced
were vastly lower than the SR-71, it was still

00:21:31.710 --> 00:21:33.549
a maintenance issue.

00:21:33.549 --> 00:21:38.380
The Concorde used a high temperature sealant
called Viton, but even this sealant hardened

00:21:38.380 --> 00:21:40.070
and cracked over time.

00:21:40.070 --> 00:21:46.950
And so, approximately every 1,100 flying hours,
roughly once a year, the Concorde was forced

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:50.929
into maintenance, which could take three weeks
to complete.

00:21:50.929 --> 00:21:55.440
Maintaining the viton sealants was one of
many procedures needed.

00:21:55.440 --> 00:22:00.480
Fuel tanks would be emptied and then vented
for 24 hours to allow technicians to climb

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:04.659
inside and manually inspect and replace sealant.

00:22:04.659 --> 00:22:10.820
This was a massive job and often required
the technicians to work with just touch as

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:17.500
access to tanks 5A and 7A on the outermost
part of the wing was impossible.

00:22:17.500 --> 00:22:21.400
The Concorde had 13 separate fuel tanks in
total.

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:27.190
Taking full advantage of the large amount
of interior volume the delta wings provided.

00:22:27.190 --> 00:22:33.139
Without these fuel tanks, capable of holding
119,000 litres of fuel [11] , the Concorde

00:22:33.139 --> 00:22:37.930
could not have made it across the Atlantic
and would have been completely useless.

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:44.320
To put that volume into perspective, a typical
Boeing 787, the world record holder for longest

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:49.500
commercial flight, can hold just 34,000 litres
of fuel.

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:54.500
These fuel tanks played a vital role in control
of the plane too.

00:22:54.500 --> 00:22:59.789
As speed increases, the centre of pressure
of a wing, the point through which all lifting

00:22:59.789 --> 00:23:03.149
force acts, tends to move backwards.

00:23:03.149 --> 00:23:05.760
:
This is usually not a significant issue for

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:11.850
slower commercial airliners, but the Concorde’s
centre of pressure moved back by as much as

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:17.620
1.8 metres. [11] More than enough to cause
significant control issues that would push

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:21.660
the plane into a nosedive dive if not accounted
for.

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:26.399
Usually, such a shift would be counteracted
by the elevator, which could stabilise the

00:23:26.399 --> 00:23:33.510
aircraft in pitch from changes in the centre
of pressure; but Concorde did not have a horizontal

00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:39.210
tail, and adding elevons on the wing big enough
to counteract this change would have resulted

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:43.150
in high levels of trim drag at such high speeds.

00:23:43.150 --> 00:23:49.160
SO, Concorde engineers instead used the huge
tanks they had at their disposal to deal with

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:50.570
this issue.

00:23:50.570 --> 00:23:55.710
As the plane approached Mach 2 fuel would
begin to be pumped out of the forward trim

00:23:55.710 --> 00:24:00.460
tanks and into the rear trim tanks and collector
tanks in the wing.

00:24:00.460 --> 00:24:06.049
Moving up to 20 tonnes of fuel rearwards and
shifting the centre of gravity backwards to

00:24:06.049 --> 00:24:09.070
match the shift in centre of pressure.

00:24:09.070 --> 00:24:14.289
Keeping the plane balanced without adding
any additional drag with control surfaces.

00:24:14.289 --> 00:24:17.649
An elegant solution, using the tools at hand

00:24:17.649 --> 00:24:20.450
The Concorde is an icon in the history of
aviation.

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:26.149
I can’t help, but feel an odd sense of nostalgia
for a plane I never got to fly in.

00:24:26.149 --> 00:24:30.840
Hundreds of thousands of passengers can see
one of the last remaining Concordes while

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:33.620
arriving and departing from Heathrow.

00:24:33.620 --> 00:24:38.679
Nicknamed Alpha Bravo, this concorde made
its last flight a month after the tragic loss

00:24:38.679 --> 00:24:40.690
of Air France 4590.

00:24:40.690 --> 00:24:46.990
It was undergoing major interior upgrades
when the decision to retire all Concordes

00:24:46.990 --> 00:24:50.889
was made, stranding the plane there indefinitely.

00:24:50.889 --> 00:24:56.620
Alpha Bravo has been in limbo ever since,
never getting to undergo the planned upgrades

00:24:56.620 --> 00:25:03.509
to prevent another disaster like Air France
4590 from ever happening again.

00:25:03.509 --> 00:25:08.470
An unintentional monument to Britain’s storied
history in aviation.

00:25:08.470 --> 00:25:12.410
Accidental monuments like this are scattered
across Britain.

00:25:12.410 --> 00:25:17.529
Gigantic, no longer functional, radar towers
can be found all over Britain.

00:25:17.529 --> 00:25:22.750
These towers were among the first radar systems
ever constructed, and played a vital role

00:25:22.750 --> 00:25:25.909
in the defence of Britain during the Battle
of Britain.

00:25:25.909 --> 00:25:31.700
A system that the Germans vastly underestimated,
thanks to not just the technology, but the

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:35.330
logistical and strategic systems that supported
it.

00:25:35.330 --> 00:25:40.090
This is the story of the latest episode of
our Battle of Britain series, that delves

00:25:40.090 --> 00:25:45.399
deep into how Britains radar systems worked
and how those systems were integrated into

00:25:45.399 --> 00:25:48.039
an effective intelligence system.

